WEBVTT

00:00:02.260 --> 00:00:02.880
Thank you.

00:00:03.820 --> 00:00:06.880
Your next question comes from the line of...

00:00:06.960 --> 00:00:08.740
James Quigley from Goldman Sachs.

00:00:08.820 --> 00:00:09.560
Please go ahead.

00:00:11.220 --> 00:00:11.440
Hello.

00:00:11.520 --> 00:00:12.300
Thank you for taking the follow-up.

00:00:12.860 --> 00:00:18.100
So earlier this year, Lutathera generics were cleared to launch by the courts in Delaware.

00:00:18.320 --> 00:00:23.140
So I think a small impact overall on the sales perspective.

00:00:23.420 --> 00:00:27.360
But how should we think about potential launches for future generic RLTs?

00:00:27.880 --> 00:00:29.500
We don't have any experience here, obviously.

00:00:30.320 --> 00:00:36.320
When thinking about generic impacts for RLTs, Novartis clearly has a number of competitive advantages.

00:00:36.600 --> 00:00:36.940
But...

00:00:37.120 --> 00:00:43.200
How are you thinking the markets could react or how could this play out if and when we see generic RLTs launching?

00:00:43.660 --> 00:00:44.080
Thank you.

00:00:44.920 --> 00:00:45.620
Yeah, thanks, James.

00:00:45.620 --> 00:00:54.540
So as far as we know, neither of the two companies has received an FDA approval.

00:00:54.700 --> 00:00:58.960
One is a 505B2 and one is a generic.

00:00:59.580 --> 00:01:06.700
We continue to believe there needs to be a high threshold used by regulators to ensure that the same dose of radiation,

00:01:06.700 --> 00:01:12.080
as being delivered to the tumor versus the originator brand that we have.

00:01:12.620 --> 00:01:22.180
Now, that being said, we do believe that given our extensive network of supply and our ability to deliver on time in full

00:01:22.180 --> 00:01:26.000
to physicians across the globe, but also across the United States,

00:01:26.320 --> 00:01:33.240
we would see an impact from a generic launch, even with a lower price being brought into the market.

00:01:33.240 --> 00:01:36.180
We think that RLT will behave very differently.

00:01:36.700 --> 00:01:42.180
Then either small molecule and potentially biosimilars just for biologics,

00:01:42.180 --> 00:01:50.640
just given the logistical complexity and as well as the expectation that physicians have that the medicine is delivered on time,

00:01:50.720 --> 00:01:54.120
each time given the nature of the logistics for the office.

00:01:55.060 --> 00:02:00.080
So, you know, we feel confident that from that said, we're not, we continue to work to keep bringing better medicine,

00:02:00.380 --> 00:02:05.660
not only with the case of PSMA and prostate cancer, but we also follow on efforts as well.

00:02:06.220 --> 00:02:06.580
For example, we have a lot of patients who have been diagnosed with PSMA and prostate cancer.

00:02:06.580 --> 00:02:06.680
We have a lot of patients who have been diagnosed with PSMA and prostate cancer.

00:02:06.680 --> 00:02:06.700
We have a lot of patients who have been diagnosed with PSMA and prostate cancer.

00:02:06.700 --> 00:02:06.760
We have a lot of patients who have been diagnosed with PSMA and prostate cancer.

00:02:06.760 --> 00:02:14.240
And really try to improve the treatment for neuroendocrine tumors as well,

00:02:14.400 --> 00:02:15.900
follow-ons for Lutathera.

00:02:16.120 --> 00:02:18.760
So stay tuned on that front as well.

00:02:19.780 --> 00:02:20.460
Great, thank you.

00:02:21.480 --> 00:02:22.320
Thank you.

00:02:23.520 --> 00:02:24.840
Cool, next question.

